// time provides points in time and the differences between them.
package "pkg/std/time"

// Delta is a signed difference between two points in time, in nanoseconds.
// It has no clock identity: once computed, a difference is just a quantity.
// For now a Delta is produced by Point.Sub and read with Nanoseconds; a Delta
// constructor and Point.Add are deliberately not provided yet.
type Delta int64

// Nanoseconds returns the difference as an exact nanosecond count.
func (d Delta) Nanoseconds() int64

// Point is a point in time on an idealized timeline — one with no leap
// seconds, extending arbitrarily into the past and the future.  It carries no
// clock identity (it is tied to no particular clock or machine); a file's
// modification time is a Point.  Comparing or subtracting two Points is
// meaningful ONLY when you know they share a source (e.g. two files on one
// filesystem): the operations treat the readings as plain numbers and cannot
// verify same-source, so that judgment is the caller's, as a raw pointer's
// validity is.
//
// Construct a Point with FromUnix — the only sanctioned constructor.  The
// fields are public only because a by-value type must publish its layout; they
// are not a supported construction path.  Ordering and Sub rely on the
// invariant that nsec is in [0, 1e9); a Point hand-built with an out-of-range
// nsec (e.g. via a composite literal) has undefined ordering.
type Point struct {
	sec  int64 // whole seconds since the year-1 origin (NOT Unix time)
	nsec int32 // sub-second part; the invariant is 0 <= nsec < 1e9
}

// FromUnix returns the Point sec seconds plus nsec nanoseconds after the Unix
// epoch (1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC).  nsec must satisfy 0 <= nsec < 1000000000;
// passing nsec outside that range panics.
func FromUnix(sec int64, nsec int32) Point

// ToUnix returns p as a Unix time — the seconds and then the nanoseconds
// after 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC (the inverse of FromUnix).  The returned
// nanoseconds are in [0, 1e9).
func (p Point) ToUnix() (int64, int32)

// SecondsSinceEpoch returns the whole seconds from Point's epoch — year 1
// (0001-01-01 00:00:00 on the idealized timeline), NOT the Unix epoch — to p.
func (p Point) SecondsSinceEpoch() int64

// Nanosecond returns p's sub-second part, in [0, 1e9).
func (p Point) Nanosecond() int32

// Before reports whether p precedes q.  Meaningful only when p and q share a
// source (see Point).
func (p Point) Before(q Point) bool

// After reports whether p follows q.  Meaningful only when p and q share a
// source (see Point).
func (p Point) After(q Point) bool

// Equal reports whether p and q are the same point.  Meaningful only when p
// and q share a source (see Point).
func (p Point) Equal(q Point) bool

// Sub returns the signed difference p - q.  Meaningful only when p and q share
// a source (see Point).  The difference must fit in a Delta — roughly ±292
// years; a larger span overflows.
func (p Point) Sub(q Point) Delta
